Quoting  John H. <JohnWH@gmail.com>:
>On movement and tactical combat interfaces: I've thought a bit about
>this.  I think those are important to a roguelike game only so far as
>they aid the item identification and usage game, which they do to a
>surprising extent.  It can certainly make a roguelike game more
>interesting, sure, but it also would allow a number of things that are
>obviously not roguelikes, or are at least absent those features that
>make them truly interesting, like Gauntlet, into the field.

I don't think unified movement and tactical combat is the _sole_
criterion; replayability through randomness is another, for example; and
although there are grey areas, I don't think my full set of criteria would
admit Gauntlet at all.

But item identification? Roguelikes don't have to have that, and indeed I
think some new attempts are damaged by adding it in unthinkingly. Kornel's
DoomRL doesn't have item ID and I say it's a roguelike.
